WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.650
Speaker 1: There's such a packed program for
you today that there's really no time to waste.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.680
For me telling you, this is a great show today.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.550
We might record this one today.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:16.090
We might actually save this one today because
the show is so packed that it would be a disservice

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:22.870
given the huge news I have to discuss with
you to spend time talking about the show in

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.400
a sort of meta sense.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.760
And so I'm not going to do that.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.310
I'm not going to talk about the show.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.949
We're just going to do the show.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:31.949
Okay.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:38.420
And at the top, we have to talk about the
collapsing polling numbers for Democrats,

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:44.030
not only in the House, we knew the House was
probably lost, but also the Senate.

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:48.680
And there are a couple of different suspicions
I have about why this is happening.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.010
Let's reset.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:55.770
Historically, in general, one party wins the
White House first midterm.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.300
It goes very poorly for that party.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.289
Bill Clinton won the White House in 1992,
1994.

00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:07.780
Historic defeat by Republicans in which Newt
Gingrich, Republican, comes to power as speaker

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:14.619
of the House and significantly moves the Republican
Party away from Reagan and towards Trump in

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:17.620
a story that we've told many times before.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:24.770
An exception to this was in 2000 and 2002
George W Bush Republican winning in 2000 Republicans

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.270
actually doing okay in 2000.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:31.509
Two considered extenuating circumstances lead
up to the Iraq war, post-9-11, etc..

00:01:31.509 --> 00:01:37.450
But generally, we would expect Democrats to
do poorly in November.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:43.350
That being said, it looked as though Democrats
were clearly going to keep control of the

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.350
Senate.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:48.990
In fact, there was a close to 70% chance,
according to the FiveThirtyEight forecast,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.329
that that indeed would happen.

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:51.329
That has changed.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:54.600
The Senate at this point is basically a coin
flip.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.770
It's a coin flip with like a slightly heavier
head side.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.659
So it's like 55, 45 that in fact, those are
the exact numbers right now.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:08.960
If you look at the FiveThirtyEight forecast,
it is a 45% chance that Republicans take the

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:09.960
Senate.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.940
55% chance that Democrats win.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:16.489
And if you scroll down the FiveThirtyEight
forecast page, which we are linking to, you

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:18.190
see the change.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.910
You see how the forecast has changed.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:26.720
And you see that after peaking right above
70% odds for Democrats in mid-September, it

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.030
has been a slow collapse and then a rapid
collapse all the way to what is now basically

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:33.129
a coin flip.

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:38.960
Now, if we look at the House, Democrats were
never favored to keep the House, but it didn't

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.340
look this grim.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:47.450
And unfortunately, the range now of outcomes
is with Republicans just barely taking the

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:54.040
House by one seat all the way to Republicans
holding a nearly 40 seat majority, Democrats

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.110
now have a 19% chance of holding the House.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.790
And if you look at how those numbers have
changed over time, sorry.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.340
Here, here it is.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:08.130
You also see that there was a similar inflection
point, at which point it became a sort of

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.989
2 to 1 disadvantage for Democrats in late
September.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:17.010
It is now down to a one in five shot for Democrats.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:22.360
Adding on to the not particularly good news,
a court has temporarily blocked Joe Biden's

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:30.019
student loan forgiveness, which was one of
the actions that Joe Biden took, which was

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:35.489
bullying Democrats and improving odds for
November.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:36.909
So what is going on here?

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:41.060
There's basically two major stories here that
I would point to.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:47.670
Number one, it is absolutely the case that
when the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:53.470
given that Americans support Roe v Wade by
a greater margin than at any point in the

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.819
Roe v Wade era, that there was anger.

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:58.030
There was anger from men, there was anger
from women.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.739
There was there was anger about that decision
because it is, after all, a right wing activist

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:04.629
Supreme Court at this time.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.879
And there was renewed interest in voting and
there was energy behind, listen, if this is

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:14.800
what these Republicans are going to do, we've
got to elect some Democrats.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.940
The timing of that energy seems to be dissipating.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:23.800
And when you now ask people what are the most
important issues, they say economy and they

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.900
say inflation, etc..

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:30.650
So the analogy I used over the weekend on
a livestream was if you are getting ready

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:37.280
for, you know, a big test at school and you
say, I'm going to I'm going to have a quadruple

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.100
express espresso, I almost said the thing.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.500
Express right now I'm going to have a quadruple
espresso and I'm going to be all fired up

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.870
and ready to go.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:51.490
And you take the espresso too early and your
energy peaks an hour before the test.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:56.050
And then by the time the test starts, the
espressos wearing off, you've mistimed the

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:58.100
surge of energy.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.100
And that is.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.400
The analogy to what I think happened here
with Roe v Wade.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.850
Now, the timing wasn't up to us.

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:06.400
The Supreme Court had its timing.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.960
And I believe that that is one fact, one factor.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:14.260
The other side is that Democratic messaging
has not been very good.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.919
And later, we're going to look at a clip from
Gavin Newsom, who says the most dangerous

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:20.770
states by by crime rates are all red states.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:25.819
And yet we let Republicans take over the narrative
and say violent Democrat states, violent Democrat

00:05:25.819 --> 00:05:26.819
state.

00:05:26.819 --> 00:05:27.819
That's just one example.

00:05:27.819 --> 00:05:30.930
But I do think the messaging from Democrats
has continued to be terrible and the messaging

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.030
from Republicans is continue to be effective.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.760
So those are two major factors.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.500
It's really not looking good.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.500
Do we give up?

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.500
Of course not.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.560
I mean, listen, if you live in Pennsylvania,
you get to vote for the Senate, you get to

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.400
vote for the governor and other local races.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.540
You might as well do it.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:47.900
And then we see what happens.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:53.250
But the numbers overall from a 30,000 foot
standpoint are looking increasingly bad for

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:59.669
Democrats, and that is very bad for the country
because of the delusional, extreme nature

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:01.500
of the modern Republican Party.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.100
And by the way, they're also starting to intimidate
voters.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.110
Let's talk about that next.

